---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const u = (p: string) => `${base}${p}`;
const news = (await getCollection('news')).sort((a, b) => b.data.date.valueOf() - a.data.date.valueOf()).slice(0, 5);
---
<Base title="unifont" description="A lightweight, cross-platform, free software font manager. Rust core, thin native shell, open standards end to end." source="site/src/pages/index.astro">
  <h1>Introduction to unifont</h1>

  <p>
    unifont is a font manager. It finds the fonts on your machine and in your own
    folders, reads what they contain, and answers questions about them: which faces
    cover Arabic, which are variable, which you are licensed to embed, which file is a
    duplicate of which. It exports what it knows as CSS, JSON and HTML specimens.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is a Rust library first, a command-line program second, and a desktop
    application third. The library and the command line exist today. The desktop
    shell is <a href={u('/roadmap/')}>planned</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is free software, released under the MIT license or the Apache License 2.0 at
    your option. It makes no network connections, collects no data, never asks for
    elevated privileges, and never writes into a system font directory.
  </p>

  <h2>Current status</h2>
  <p>
    Version 0.0.1, pre-release. The core and the command line parse TTF, OTF, TTC, WOFF
    and WOFF2; build a searchable SQLite index; group faces into families from the
    typographic name records and the <code>STAT</code> table; run health checks; report
    licenses as SPDX identifiers; tag faces, keep collections and register source
    directories; and export <code>@font-face</code> rules and self-contained HTML
    specimens. Font activation on Linux, macOS and Windows is the next milestone.
  </p>
  <p>
    "unifont" is a working name. It collides with GNU Unifont, the bitmap font, and will
    change before the first release.
  </p>

  <h2>Principles</h2>
  <p>These are not aspirations. They are checked in continuous integration or refused in review.</p>
  <dl>
    <dt>Correct</dt>
    <dd>Every byte of OpenType is parsed by <a href="https://github.com/googlefonts/fontations">fontations</a>,
      the parser Chrome and Skia use. Families come from name IDs 16 and 17 and from
      <code>STAT</code>, never from file names.</dd>
    <dt>Standard</dt>
    <dd>The style model is CSS Fonts Level 4. Licenses are SPDX. Paths follow the XDG
      Base Directory specification. Every export is JSON with a published JSON Schema.
      Configuration is TOML.</dd>
    <dt>Light</dt>
    <dd>Install size, start time and idle memory have hard budgets. A build that
      exceeds them fails.</dd>
    <dt>Private</dt>
    <dd>No network calls in the core or the command line. No telemetry, ever. Catalog
      features, when they come, are opt-in and live only in the desktop application.</dd>
    <dt>Per-user</dt>
    <dd>System font directories are never modified. Nothing requires elevation.</dd>
    <dt>Reproducible</dt>
    <dd>Releases carry SHA-256 checksums, SLSA build provenance attestations and an SPDX
      software bill of materials.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Getting it</h2>
  <p>
    See <a href={u('/download/')}>Download</a> for release binaries and how to verify
    them, or build from source with a Rust toolchain of version 1.88 or newer:
  </p>
  <pre><code>cargo install --git https://github.com/oddurs/unifont unifont-cli</code></pre>

  <h2>Documentation</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={u('/docs/')}>The unifont manual</a>, including the
      <a href={u('/docs/cli/')}>command reference</a> in the form of a man page.</li>
    <li><a href={u('/faq/')}>Frequently asked questions</a>.</li>
    <li><a href={u('/adr/')}>Architecture decision records</a>: why fontations, why SQLite, why a webview.</li>
    <li><a href={u('/roadmap/')}>Roadmap</a>: what exists, what is next, what is out of scope.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Recent news</h2>
  <ul>
    {news.map((n) => (
      <li>{n.data.date.toISOString().slice(0, 10)}: <a href={u(`/news/${n.id}/`)}>{n.data.title}</a></li>
    ))}
  </ul>
  <p><a href={u('/news/')}>All news</a>, also as an <a href={u('/feed.xml')}>RSS feed</a>.</p>

  <h2>Help wanted</h2>
  <p>
    unifont is developed in the open at
    <a href="https://github.com/oddurs/unifont">github.com/oddurs/unifont</a>.
    Bug reports, fixture fonts under an open license, and platform testing on Linux
    desktops are the most useful contributions right now. Read
    <a href={u('/contributing/')}>Contributing</a> first.
  </p>
</Base>
